INTRODUCTION
Stiff person syndrome (SPS) is a rare 
neurological  disorder of unclear aetiology. 
It is characterized by progressive rigidity 
and stiffness primarily affecting the truncal 
muscles and is superimposed by spasms and 
postural deformities.
1 It frequently affects people 
in their 40s but can occur between 30-60years. 2 
It is unique because it lacks similarity to any 
other neurologic disorder. SPS is extremely 
rare with less than 20 cases reported from 
South Asia which has a population of nearly 2 
billion.
3 Muscle rigidity in SPS waxes and wanes 
with concurrent spasms. The pathophysiology 
of the disease is autoimmune. 4 The most 
common pathologic correlate is anti–glutamic 
acid decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies which 
is associated with the idiopathic form.
4 The 
paraneoplastic form which comprises around 5% 
was reported with malignancies of the breast, 
colon, lung, and thymus.
